  "summary": "Centuries-old trees kept small by pot, wire and patience — landscapes compressed to a silhouette.",
  "description": "Bonsai grows full trees — maples, pines, junipers — whose scale lies: a thirty-year trunk of two hands' width, branch pads wired and pruned into cloud tiers, roots nebari gripping the soil like a cliff-edge. From Chinese penjing brought through Zen temples to Japan, bonsai became a disciplined art of suggestion: the tree implies wind, age, mountain. Seasonal work — wiring in winter, pinching candles in spring — is a lifelong calendar; display pairs the tree with a scroll, an accent plant and the right pot in the tokonoma.",
